    "evidence": [
      {
        "evidence_id": "E001",
        "passage_id": "P001",
        "quote": "In an action to recover damages for personal injuries, the defendant appeals from an order of the Supreme Court, Kings County (Lawrence Knipel, J.), dated July 30, 2018. The order, insofar as appealed from, denied the defendant's motion for summary judgment dismissing the complaint.",
        "statement_status": "procedural_history"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "E002",
        "passage_id": "P001",
        "quote": "Ordered that the order is affirmed insofar as appealed from, with costs.",
        "statement_status": "current_disposition"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "E003",
        "passage_id": "P001",
        "quote": "The plaintiff allegedly was injured when she tripped and fell on a raised floor divider that separated the carpet from the linoleum floor at premises owned by the defendant and leased to the plaintiff's employer.",
        "statement_status": "allegation"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "E004",
        "passage_id": "P003",
        "quote": "Here, the defendant demonstrated, prima facie, that it was an out-of-possession landlord, that it was not contractually obligated to maintain the carpeting, that it did not assume such a duty through a course of conduct, and that it did not create the alleged defect by installing the carpeting or the floor divider",
        "statement_status": "court_finding"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "E005",
        "passage_id": "P003",
        "quote": "However, the evidence submitted in opposition to the motion, which included the affidavits of the plaintiff and two of her former coworkers, raised a triable issue of fact as to whether the defendant assumed a duty to maintain the carpeting by its course of conduct",
        "statement_status": "court_finding"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "E006",
        "passage_id": "P004",
        "quote": "or created the alleged defect by replacing the carpeting a few months before the plaintiff's fall.",
        "statement_status": "court_finding"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "E007",
        "passage_id": "P004",
        "quote": "Furthermore, the defendant failed to demonstrate, prima facie, that it did not have constructive notice of the allegedly defective condition that caused the plaintiff to fall",
        "statement_status": "court_finding"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "E008",
        "passage_id": "P004",
        "quote": "The defendant's contention that the affidavits of the plaintiff's former coworkers should not have been considered was improperly raised for the first time on appeal in its reply brief",
        "statement_status": "court_finding"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "E009",
        "passage_id": "P005",
        "quote": "and the photographs upon which the defendant relied were not authenticated by evidence sufficient to establish that the condition at the time of the plaintiff's fall was substantially as shown in the photograph",
        "statement_status": "court_finding"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "E010",
        "passage_id": "P004",
        "quote": "In addition, the defendant failed to establish, prima facie, that the alleged defect was trivial and, therefore, not actionable ( see Hutchinson v Sheridan Hill House Corp. , 26 NY3d 66 , 77-79 [2015]; Trincere v County of Suffolk , 90 NY2d 976, 977 [1997]). In this regard, the defendant failed to eliminate all triable issues of fact as to the dimensions of the alleged defect",
        "statement_status": "court_finding"
      },
      {
        "evidence_id": "E011",
        "passage_id": "P005",
        "quote": "Accordingly, we agree with the Supreme Court's determination to deny the defendant's motion for summary judgment dismissing the complaint.",
        "statement_status": "court_finding"
      }
    ],
